<nav class="bread" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="{{ route('home') }}">Home</a> / <a href="{{ route('categories.credit') }}">Credit</a> / <span aria-current="page">Credit card interest: the details that change your cost</span></nav><div class="article-layout"><article><header class="article-header"><span class="eyebrow">Credit / EXPLAINER</span><h1>Credit card interest: the details that change your cost</h1><p class="deck">Understand grace periods, balances, and why minimum payments stretch repayment.</p><div class="byline"><span class="avatar" aria-hidden="true">FH</span><div><a href="{{ route('authors.editorial-team') }}"><strong>FinancersHub editorial team</strong></a><br><span class="meta">Editorial preview · Educational guide</span></div></div><div class="share"><button class="btn ghost" type="button" data-copy-link>Copy link</button></div></header><div class="art gold article-hero"><img src="{{ asset('assets/images/responsive/editorial-personal-finance-640.webp') }}" srcset="{{ asset('assets/images/responsive/editorial-personal-finance-320.webp') }} 320w, {{ asset('assets/images/responsive/editorial-personal-finance-640.webp') }} 640w, {{ asset('assets/images/responsive/editorial-personal-finance-876.webp') }} 876w" sizes="(max-width: 600px) 100vw, (max-width: 1100px) 60vw, 800px" alt="" loading="eager" width="800" height="500"></div><p class="caption">Original editorial image for the FinancersHub design preview.</p><div class="prose"><p class="article-opening">A credit card’s annual percentage rate is only one part of its borrowing cost. The balance you carry, the way payments are applied, and any grace period all affect what you may pay.</p><section class="article-takeaways" aria-label="Questions to keep in mind"><span class="eyebrow">KEEP IN MIND</span><h2>Three questions to ask</h2><ol><li><span>01</span>Does a purchase grace period apply to you?</li><li><span>02</span>What happens if you pay only the minimum?</li><li><span>03</span>Which rate and fees apply to this transaction?</li></ol></section><section class="guide-section" id="section-1"><h2>Find out when interest starts</h2><p>A grace period is the time between the end of a billing cycle and the payment due date. Many cards offer one on purchases when the statement balance is paid in full by the due date, but issuers are not required to provide it. Check the agreement for your card and for different types of transactions.</p></section><section class="guide-section" id="section-2"><h2>Look beyond the minimum payment</h2><p>A minimum payment can keep an account current while leaving a balance that continues to accrue interest. Read the payment information on your statement and compare what changes if you pay more. Avoid treating a lower monthly payment as a lower total cost.</p></section><section class="guide-section" id="section-3"><h2>Review the actual agreement</h2><p>Check the purchase APR, other APRs, fees, and any promotional terms. If you have carried a balance, ask the issuer when interest will stop after you pay it off. Rules vary by account, and your statement is the place to start.</p></section><section class="article-source" aria-labelledby="source-title"><span class="eyebrow">SOURCE &amp; FURTHER READING</span><h2 id="source-title">Check the primary source</h2><p>The guide is newly written for this preview and draws its factual background from <a href="https://www.consumerfinance.gov/ask-cfpb/what-is-a-grace-period-for-a-credit-card-en-47/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Consumer Financial Protection Bureau: Credit card grace periods ↗</a>. Check that source and current product terms before making a decision.</p><ul><li><a href="https://www.consumerfinance.gov/consumer-tools/credit-cards/answers/key-terms/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Consumer Financial Protection Bureau: Credit card terms ↗</a></li></ul></section><div class="disclosure"><strong>Educational preview.</strong> This draft is general information for U.S. readers, not personal financial, investment, tax, or legal advice. It needs final editorial and rights review before publication.</div></div><section class="article-author-card" aria-labelledby="author-title"><span class="avatar" aria-hidden="true">FH</span><div><span class="eyebrow">ABOUT THE AUTHOR</span><h2 id="author-title">FinancersHub editorial team</h2><p>Shared attribution for this design preview. Individual writer profiles and credentials will be added after verification.</p><a class="text-link" href="{{ route('authors.editorial-team') }}">View profile ↗</a></div></section><section class="section article-more"><div class="section-head"><div><span class="eyebrow">FROM THE LIBRARY</span><h2>Continue reading</h2></div></div><div class="article-related-grid"><article class="card"><a href="{{ route('articles.emergency-fund') }}" aria-label="Read A clearer way to build an emergency fund"><div class="art teal "><img src="{{ asset('assets/images/responsive/editorial-personal-finance-640.webp') }}" srcset="{{ asset('assets/images/responsive/editorial-personal-finance-320.webp') }} 320w, {{ asset('assets/images/responsive/editorial-personal-finance-640.webp') }} 640w, {{ asset('assets/images/responsive/editorial-personal-finance-876.webp') }} 876w" sizes="(max-width: 600px) 100vw, (max-width: 1100px) 60vw, 800px" alt="" loading="lazy" width="800" height="500"></div></a><div class="body"><a class="eyebrow" href="{{ route('categories.personal-finance') }}">Personal Finance</a><h3><a href="{{ route('articles.emergency-fund') }}">A clearer way to build an emergency fund</a></h3><p>A practical framework for choosing a target, setting a pace, and keeping cash available when you need it.</p><span class="meta">Guide</span></div></article><article class="card"><a href="{{ route('articles.index-funds') }}" aria-label="Read How index funds work, in plain English"><div class="art blue "><img src="{{ asset('assets/images/responsive/editorial-investing-640.webp') }}" srcset="{{ asset('a